<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brasília</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brasília is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, built on the central plateau and inaugurated on 21 April 1960 under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juscelino-kubitschek">Juscelino Kubitschek</a>. It was planned by Lúcio Costa, with major buildings by Oscar Niemeyer, to move government away from the coast and open up the interior. Its modernist plan is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brasilia.jpg" alt="Brasília" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arturdiasr · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Planalto_Central_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Brasília sits at an elevation of 1,000 metres on the Brazilian Highlands in the Central-West region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Paranoá Lake, a large artificial lake, was built to increase the available water and to maintain the region's humidity. The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation of Brazil, suggested for the country's capital in 1821 by José Bonifácio de Andrada e Silva.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s first capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>, and in 1763 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> became the capital and remained so until 1960. Resources tended to be concentrated in the southeast, and most of the population lived near the Atlantic coast. A geographically central capital would be more regionally neutral. The idea of moving the capital was conceived in 1827 by José Bonifácio, an adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Emperor Pedro I</a>, who presented a plan to the General Assembly for a new city called Brasília, to the west of the heavily populated southeastern corridor. It was to stand upstream on the São Francisco River rather than in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goias-velho">Goiás</a> chosen later. The bill was not enacted because Pedro I dissolved the Assembly.</p><p>According to a legend, the Italian saint Don Bosco dreamed in 1883 of a futuristic city that roughly fitted Brasília's location, and many references to him, including a church parish, are found in the city today. After the Proclamation of the Republic, the third article of the 1891 Constitution stated that the Union would own, in the central plateau, an area of 14,400 square kilometres, to be demarcated to establish the future federal capital.</p><p>The city was developed in 1956 by Lúcio Costa, Oscar Niemeyer and Joaquim Cardozo, in a scheme to move the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> to a more central location chosen through a committee, with Roberto Burle Marx as landscape architect. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juscelino-kubitschek">Juscelino Kubitschek</a> founded it on 21 April 1960 to replace Rio de Janeiro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Brasília is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and of the Federal District, and all three branches of the federal government sit there. It is laid out in the shape of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeroplane">airplane</a>: the fuselage is the Monumental Axis, a pair of wide avenues flanking a large park, and the cockpit is the Praça dos Três Poderes. Its white modern architecture is by Oscar Niemeyer. UNESCO inscribed it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1987 for its modernist architecture and urban planning. It is an administrative region rather than a municipality, and the Federal District is divided into 35 administrative regions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brasília” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Built in under four years to plans by Lúcio Costa and Oscar Niemeyer, <b>Brasília</b> replaced Rio de Janeiro as capital.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-21">21 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 20th century its territory hosted the new national capital, <b>Brasília</b>, and the planned city of Goiânia replaced Goiás Velho as state capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-goias">Captaincy of Goiás</a></li><li><blockquote>Juscelino Kubitschek promised 'fifty years in five' and built the new capital, <b>Brasília</b>, inaugurated in 1960.</blockquote><a href="/state/fourth-brazilian-republic">Fourth Brazilian Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Jair Bolsonaro governed from 2019 to 2022 and lost to Lula; Bolsonaro's supporters stormed government buildings in <b>Brasília</b> on 8 January 2023.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-republic-brazil">New Republic (Brazil)</a></li></ul></section>
